based on the truth of a hypothesis that every 3-connected 3-regular planar graph is hamiltonian. The proof, however, turned out to be false and the first counterexample to the hypothesis, that is, a non-hamiltonian 3-connected 3-regular planar graph, was constructed by Tutte [24] in 1946. Later, 3-connected 3-regular planar graphs with no Hamilton paths were also found [12].\n\nOn the positive side, Tutte [25] showed in 1956 that every 4-connected planar graph does have a Hamilton cycle. Tutte’s result was strengthened by Thomassen [23] who proved that every 4-connected planar graph is hamiltonian-connected, that is, any two vertices are connected by some Hamilton path.\n\nBarnette [1] showed that every 3-connected planar graph has a 3-tree. Confirming a conjecture of Jackson and Wormald [14] and strengthening Barnette’s result, Gao and Richter [8] proved that every 3-connected planar graph has in fact a 2-walk (see also [9, 10]). Hence, it is natural to ask if all 3-connected planar graphs can reach the level of “Hamilton cycle in prism” in the hamiltonian property hierarchy. This question was formulated as a conjecture by Kaiser, Ryjáček, Král’, Rosenfeld and Voss [15], which was also attributed to Rosenfeld and Barnette (see [11] and [20]).\n\n**Conjecture 1** ([15, Conjecture 1]). Every 3-connected planar graph has a hamiltonian prism.\n\nTo support this conjecture, the following graph classes were shown to be prism-hamiltonian: 3-connected 3-regular (not necessarily planar) graphs [19], Halin graphs [15], 3-connected bipartite planar graphs [2], near-triangulations [2] and 3-connected planar graphs with minimum degree at least four [21]. However, a recent breakthrough by Špacapan [20] showed that the conjecture does not hold in general. Based on Špacapan’s technique, Ikegami, Maezawa and Zamfirescu [13] provided various classes of counterexamples with some special properties.\n\n**Theorem 2** ([20]). *There are infinitely many 3-connected planar non-prism-hamiltonian graphs.*\n\nA good cactus is a connected graph in which every block is either an edge or a cycle and every vertex is contained in one or two blocks. A good cactus is even if it is bipartite (see\n\nTHE ELECTRONIC JOURNAL OF COMBINATORICS 29(4) (2022), #P4.18  2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInducibility of 4-vertex tournaments (https://lidicky.name/pub/tournaments/Tournaments.pdf)\nciteturn5search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Figure 2: For odd n, the carousel C ∈Cn is unique and vertex transitive. ... Conjecture 3. ... probabilistic construction of a tournament ˜Tn on n vertices which was discovered independently by\nN−(v)\nN+(v)\nTransitive\nTransitive\nv\nFigure 2: For odd n, the carousel C ∈Cn is unique and vertex transitive. For even n, the directions\nof the diagonals can be chosen arbitrarily, resulting in several non-isomorphic tournaments.\nConjecture 3. For all k ≥5 and n ≥k, the unique n-vertex tournaments maximizing I(Ck,T) are\nthe tournaments Cn.\nThe only tournaments on 4 vertices left to consider are the two tournaments C−\n3 and C+\n3 . As\none gets C−\n3 from C+\n3 by reversal of all arcs, the tournaments extremal for C−\n3 are precisely the re-\nversals of the tournaments extremal for C+\n3 , so it suffices to only study C+\n3 . Consider the following\nprobabilistic construction of a tournament ˜Tn on n vertices which was discovered independently by\nBurgher and Burke, and in [4] with an almost matching upper bound via the flag algebra method.\nFor some fixed α ∈(0,1), partition the vertices into two sets Hn (for high out-degree) and Ln (for\nlow out-degree) of size ⌈αn⌉and ⌊(1 −α)n⌋, respectively. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHamilton cycles in prisms (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=f8c05a907898a44b256f78bcbcb818708f2d08b9&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn5search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; Any 3